<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercises xml:id="ex_first" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Exercise</title>

   <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Estimate the total internal energy of 1.00 L of nitrogen (<m>N_2</m>) gas at 298K and 2 atm if its temperature is increased 
          by 10K at constant pressure. What is the energy required to heat 1.00 mol of nitrogen gas 
          from <m>0 K</m> to <m>298 K</m> at constant pressure?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
         
        <p>
           To estimate the total internal energy of 1.00 L of <m>N_2</m> gas at 298 K and 2 atm, 
           we can use the ideal gas law to find the number of moles of nitrogen gas. 
          we then calculate the internal energy by
          <me>
            dU = n\frac{5}{2} R(T_2-T_1) 
          </me>
          From Ideal gas equation, we can find the number of moles of nitrogen gas: 
          <me>
            n= \frac{PV}{RT} = \frac{(2 \, \text{atm})(1.00 \, \text{L})}{(0.0821 \, \text{L atm/mol K})(298 \, \text{K})} \approx 0.0816 \, \text{mol} 
          </me>
          or, 
            <me>
            \Delta U = 0.0816 \, \text{mol} \times \frac{5}{2} (8.314 \, \text{J/mol K}) (10.0 \, \text{K}) \approx 17.0 \, \text{J} 
          </me>  
          The energy required to heat 1.00 mol of nitrogen gas from 0 K to 298 K at constant pressure can be calculated using the same formula:
          <me>
            \Delta U = 1.00 \, \text{mol} \times \frac{5}{2} (8.314 \, \text{J/mol K}) (298 \, \text{K} - 0 \, \text{K}) \approx 6200 \, \text{J} 
          </me>  
        </p>
      </solution>
     </example>
      
</exercises>


